To write and run C++ code, you need a C++ compiler (which turns your code into an executable program) and a code editor or IDE. Because installation steps vary by operating system, choose the guide below for your device.

Windows

You have two main options on Windows depending on whether you want a full-featured development suite or a lightweight editor.
• Option 1: All-in-One Setup (Visual Studio Community). Download and run the installer from the Visual Studio Downloads page. During setup, check the Desktop development with C++ workload and click Install. This automatically configures both the editor and the Microsoft MSVC compiler.
• Option 2: Lightweight Setup (VS Code + MinGW-w64).
	1. Install Visual Studio Code and add the C/C++ extension from the Extensions view (Ctrl+Shift+X).
	2. Download and install MSYS2 to get the GCC compiler.
	3. Open the MSYS2 terminal and run pacman -S --needed base-devel mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-toolchain.
	4. Add the compiler's bin folder (typically C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin) to your Windows System Path environment variable.
• Verification. Open Command Prompt or PowerShell and run g++ --version (for MinGW) to confirm the compiler is recognized
